  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "Both now exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't confirmed that the rule runs cleanly: its status is still \"pending\", because it hasn't had its first run yet.\n\n**What the data holds:** the Kibana Sample Data Logs view covers web-server access logs. The main fields are:\n- `timestamp`\n- `response` (a string, so I used `response.keyword`)\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `clientip`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `request`, `url` and `referer`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-c57cabaf Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- **Total requests:** a single-number count of all requests.\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart of request counts by `timestamp`.\n- **Requests by response code:** a pie chart split by `response.keyword`.\n- **Average bytes by extension:** a bar chart of average `bytes` per file extension.\n\nIt is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-c57cabaf-web-logs-overview-5892e0f2623f.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-c57cabaf 5xx spike\" (id `6fddf17b-b2d3-4435-8529-f76970015e71`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It looks at `kibana_sample_data_logs` over the last 5 minutes. It fires when more than 10 documents have a `response.keyword` starting with \"5\".\n\nI didn't attach any notification action, so when it fires it will show up as an alert in Kibana but won't notify anyone.",
